Same prefix-doubling suffix sort and the same data. The reference's `sort_by` is a library stable sort (driftsort) over the suffix array; here the language's `sort` (a library merge sort in the runtime that skips the merge of halves already in order) sorts one 64-bit key per suffix: its pair of ranks above its position in the last round's order, so equal pairs keep that order as in a stable sort (library against library, but a different algorithm from the reference's). The suffixes are taken in the last round's order when the keys are built, which is what makes the data nearly sorted (built in text order the sort took 1.57 s). The reference allocates a pairs vector and a new rank vector every round; here the keys, the scratch array the sort works in and the second suffix array are state arrays, and the new ranks overwrite the old. State is unlogged, so nothing is saved for a rollback. main_merge.mito is the earlier version with a merge sort written in the language (0.54 s).
